aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087699 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b9ef325-2358-5041-8a8d-b459e8ed9f09" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d32226b-4a5c-5ca1-a94c-eb0a19d093db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235889} {:answer/id #uuid "1c0d83e4-9c45-5545-a833-b798417f2329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235996} {:answer/id #uuid "621f1133-add7-5cb0-b027-86691e81ad44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236439} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4d8c1d-193e-53ee-8b93-897b2c4bda25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236711} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d75378-7dcf-50aa-9f3b-e25d4b3f4372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1d75378-7dcf-50aa-9f3b-e25d4b3f4372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237143} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b5de887-4a19-59e0-af28-660cabc75781" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7fa0b65-e3f9-5815-a23d-64900cd2d836" :question/math-academy-id "q-102675" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n- $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n- $B\\ne {A}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false. As a result, $B\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a354e63-8613-53db-87e2-25a664b37cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236080} {:answer/id #uuid "2b12a7fb-4d6f-544a-a96f-ed35aacbc53d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236086} {:answer/id #uuid "5a87be2c-5994-5264-a776-dae0ae4f4cbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236383} {:answer/id #uuid "866e96f5-4b4a-5959-b121-cdb20b47a301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236653} {:answer/id #uuid "b84cde27-376f-5d07-afc4-85d5372677dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b12a7fb-4d6f-544a-a96f-ed35aacbc53d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236086} :answer-field/id #uuid "6701e43f-c4b2-5b9b-813e-73e8f05c1bee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "343b3c1e-da90-580a-91f6-9c9756fee1a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-103150" :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x+3)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-1,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=-5$\n- The series converges at $x=-2$\n- The series converges at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=-3,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-1,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $2,$ i.e., $R\\ge 2.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-5,-1].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-5,-1].$\n\n![](images/7d/7d68a4bd81717c3ad663fc85c45dfce62217a046f683cc92a6869f87ff3b166a.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-5\\notin (-5,-1],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $-2\\in (-5,-1],$ the series is convergent at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $1\\notin (-5,-1],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c9471e0-137a-536a-bdea-8ab035f96e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236304} {:answer/id #uuid "56e12010-1575-5bf0-9418-58ea47ea235b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236367} {:answer/id #uuid "b7810ba6-1de7-51b9-81a4-8ad9b2127163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236983} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ce66c2-6b9a-5bcb-9a04-c36c7ad048e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237189} {:answer/id #uuid "ea8eb5cb-fe1d-5506-ad9f-b52d18520a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7810ba6-1de7-51b9-81a4-8ad9b2127163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236983} :answer-field/id #uuid "330cfe04-73b4-5fe7-9498-423d1a019e86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21ccde81-8669-5255-b2ed-8bdddd923bd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-103152" :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-5)^{n}$ converges at  $x=2,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series diverges at $x=6$\n- The series converges at $x=0$\n- The series converges at $x=8$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=5,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=2,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $3,$ i.e., $R\\ge 3.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[2,8).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[2,8).$\n\n![](images/df/dfd59d22892764c4da387d48b07a6a1069ea59e13be75cd099d21ce53975d64c.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $6\\in [2,8),$ the series is convergent at $x=6.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $0\\notin [2,8),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $8\\notin [2,8),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the listed.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4deaa6-1045-5a3c-a073-139ae1ce42c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235882} {:answer/id #uuid "57a89d03-252b-5807-a5c8-42501698e359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236372} {:answer/id #uuid "b624e040-e7fb-58e5-825e-152465176a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236968} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e2031a-2dc6-5a9f-99ef-9b3f95c11fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236986} {:answer/id #uuid "fd67b321-1d90-5bda-a2d0-26a61617da7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c4deaa6-1045-5a3c-a073-139ae1ce42c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235882} :answer-field/id #uuid "4de57f02-8a1e-5f60-8335-889ffbcc2c06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "93c0e2ef-7f5e-5e9b-aac8-f784a726661a" :question/math-academy-id "q-103169" :question/problem "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{n}$ converges at  $x=-1.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=2$\n- The series converges at $x=1$\n- The series converges at $x=4$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-1,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $\\frac{5}{2},$ i.e., $R\\ge \\frac{5}{2}.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-1,4).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-1,4).$\n\n![](images/d0/d01788625c7b429a15caf441c6a651fa92281dc544f99c544bdb12f9d8b6f032.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $2\\in [-1,4),$ the series is convergent at $x=2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $1\\in [-1,4),$ the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement III is not necessarily true. The given information guarantees convergence for points strictly between $-1$ and $4$, but it does not guarantee convergence at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d7d6e3d-fae5-5c77-8600-1c3cd9256323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236313} {:answer/id #uuid "7028432e-24cd-5f8a-a92a-aec62cef0c14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236526} {:answer/id #uuid "706dafcd-5568-5572-8b00-971cb5925392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236527} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb443a9-a2ba-5536-919e-1755e5d3718b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236595} {:answer/id #uuid "ff30d3ae-e93f-52a5-8c4c-c20e0596c02e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d7d6e3d-fae5-5c77-8600-1c3cd9256323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236313} :answer-field/id #uuid "33a22350-f913-543a-a22a-50b96be455dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "76850d0b-0e5d-5926-80f5-97253aff4b0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103170" :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x+7)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-3,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=-11$\n- The series converges at $x=-8$\n- The series converges at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=-7,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-3,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $4,$ i.e., $R\\ge 4.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-11,-3].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-11,-3].$\n\n![](images/47/4764256e5f00e7a11d688bd99d5fa1c22db819e7a188113f0ef4cd987a9b7595.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-11\\notin (-11,-3],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $-8\\in (-11,-3],$ the series is convergent at $x=-8.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $1\\notin (-11,-3],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147499 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24115d7a-5a6a-5584-a664-89b0856ff10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236048} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0f403e-4aee-503b-bfb5-277bc79a0f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236183} {:answer/id #uuid "3c15596e-43ae-585e-8959-c82bca97affb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236192} {:answer/id #uuid "72bf4c08-6663-5e3d-b09c-5f3e53e1f0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236544} {:answer/id #uuid "cb25a744-60e0-5136-bf9b-d043a4bd32eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a0f403e-4aee-503b-bfb5-277bc79a0f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236183} :answer-field/id #uuid "54c67153-b5bb-5f13-b369-17f7dd25f4e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59bba89f-5fae-5f25-a8be-5a25060edfd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-103172" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)$\n- $3{y}^{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂x}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $Q(x,y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y).$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2})=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1))=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)$ gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147499 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c73fb72-7bad-55b7-8df0-3564802eedd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236002} {:answer/id #uuid "3c78c80b-7c7c-538e-a803-cd22fbfa240a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236196} {:answer/id #uuid "54af3b0f-3606-50e6-9b43-8824c1d473f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236357} {:answer/id #uuid "8030e282-4115-5ea7-98a9-7a6ebe8548d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236614} {:answer/id #uuid "c4cde532-81c0-5acf-b2b1-d8e6bf3bed05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4cde532-81c0-5acf-b2b1-d8e6bf3bed05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237061} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f790423-e118-5521-8bd6-4906f2ebe62c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d411ceca-268e-5683-adfb-764387aebbdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-103174" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n2x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $6x{y}^{2}$\n- $3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$\n- $2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y)=2x{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{3})=6x{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂x}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{2})=6{y}^{2}\\ne \\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6x{y}^{2}=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2})=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\ne \\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''II only''."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147499 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f28d1f-9ec9-5aae-972b-44e57c0e305f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236168} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcb5535-d83e-5bca-9145-03d3fe4428f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236314} {:answer/id #uuid "6da1fdd4-3e85-5519-aa71-65b5414883f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236510} {:answer/id #uuid "e673ed0b-4cea-54f6-a6d2-9141063186e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237265} {:answer/id #uuid "fdea57f7-2fa9-5ebc-a831-3f35becaa981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dcb5535-d83e-5bca-9145-03d3fe4428f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236314} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd3af6b5-6b6a-5486-986b-25a843b240ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ffb478e-6d0e-5395-bbb6-0e1bbace63ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-103175" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\n- ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}$\n- $-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$\n- ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2})=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2})=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''I only''."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc2ce46-b2a6-56de-8ead-93675bda43d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236005} {:answer/id #uuid "86f59763-455d-5124-95ad-635362b1efd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236659} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3a6054-cf44-59c2-a27b-e541dd8834c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236710} {:answer/id #uuid "e741a1bd-1d6c-5121-875f-22abed90b858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237269} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe8aa25-895a-5b00-b727-a03ec675ff51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cc2ce46-b2a6-56de-8ead-93675bda43d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236005} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a50de25-7441-55af-a64b-83272a0b732e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4817b633-1cdd-52d9-b9a7-098236a7867f" :question/math-academy-id "q-103230" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cos⁡x?$\n\n- The function is zero at $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n- Its range is $y\\in [-2,2]$\n- $y=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is an output of the function" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. At $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$, the function $y=cos⁡x$ takes the value zero.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. The range of the function $y=cos⁡x$ is $y\\in [-1,1].$ Thus, $y=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is not an output of this function."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13cf2350-5cb8-5bce-82d7-7d69855b39b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235935} {:answer/id #uuid "3656391b-7c11-597f-a2bd-8b6a0f95faaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236162} {:answer/id #uuid "431b8b2c-60f9-50ed-a73e-335785cc18ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236250} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c131e4-70f4-5b57-9ffb-981148d71879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236948} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d7012c-5356-5c36-b422-e156f9cbdec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2c131e4-70f4-5b57-9ffb-981148d71879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236948} :answer-field/id #uuid "49b37897-7ab2-5034-96e3-b9f2f4798c3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4afb86c8-970f-594e-bb88-36f6e2c3e725" :question/math-academy-id "q-103233" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cos⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The zeros of the function are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi $\n- Its graph passes through the point $(0,1)$\n- The function has maximum values at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The zeros occur at $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…,$ which can be written as $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is true. At $x=0,$ the function has the value $y=cos⁡0=1.$ Therefore, the graph of the function passes through the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a maximum at $x=0,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=2n\\pi .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241c7bd-4a2b-556f-be9f-8a884addebb9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c2f2bf1-9489-53d0-aef7-7228b981bac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235998} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff2b5ac-aa6d-5187-84fd-8accf825947a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236023} {:answer/id #uuid "4126e729-233b-53a1-875e-c58b84df84ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236232} {:answer/id #uuid "75f6292f-0f0c-5b71-9614-126a4b2d68e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236556} {:answer/id #uuid "83034a3e-d469-513a-b703-047d7a3470a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c2f2bf1-9489-53d0-aef7-7228b981bac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235998} :answer-field/id #uuid "92061e84-03fe-54ba-9008-b9fd45a3987b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2215b590-8140-5323-92bf-8ae24e1b7bb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-103289" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the function is all real numbers except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $\n- The function has zeros at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $ for any integer $n$\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/73/732955302ca49a41f6c43cf242d7c869e630e88d804c4b56a5beb3b081f2d230.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is defined everywhere, except where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function takes values on all the real numbers, hence the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}]
